# Docker does NOT read .gitignore. Anything excluded there is still sent to the build and still
# lands in the image unless it is named again HERE.
#
# This file exists because that bit: the repo's .gitignore excludes .venv, so a developer venv sat
# untracked in the working tree - and `COPY . /opt/agent-boot` cheerfully copied it into the image,
# absolute host paths and all. The build then failed with "a virtual environment already exists",
# which reads as a uv quirk and is actually "your laptop is inside the container now".
#
# Two costs, and the quiet one is worse: the build breaks (loud, fixable), and host paths plus
# whatever else is lying around untracked ship inside a published image (silent, permanent).

# --- environments and caches -----------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# --- vcs and local state ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.forgejo/

# --- editor / os noise -------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
*.swp

# --- nothing secret should exist here, but say so explicitly ----------------------------------
# An image is published; a working tree is not. If any of these ever appear locally they must not
# be the thing that discovers the difference.
.env
*.pem
*.key
!examples/**/*.kdl
secrets/
state/
